---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import HeroJourney from '../components/HeroJourney.astro';
import Examples from '../components/Examples.astro';
import ProviderStrip from '../components/ProviderStrip.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import brandLogo from '../assets/logo_dumbdiagrams_highres.png';
import websitePackage from '../../package.json';
import { url, npmUrl, repository } from '../lib/site';
const releaseVersion = websitePackage.version;
const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Dumb Diagrams', applicationCategory: 'DeveloperApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Windows, macOS, Linux', runtimePlatform: 'Node.js 24+',
  description: 'LLM-first architecture diagrams as code with portable .dgm source, deterministic layout, and a local visual editor.',
  license: 'https://spdx.org/licenses/AGPL-3.0-only.html',
  isAccessibleForFree: true, downloadUrl: npmUrl,
  ...(Astro.site ? { url: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href } : {}),
  ...(repository ? { sameAs: repository } : {}),
};
---
<Base structuredData={structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow"><i></i> LLM-FIRST ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAMS</span><span class="version mono">OPEN SOURCE / v{releaseVersion}</span></div>
      <div class="hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <h1 id="hero-title">A little opinionated.<br /><span>A lot clearer<span class="hero-period">.</span></span></h1>
      <div class="hero-description"><p>Architecture diagrams as code for AI-first teams. Let an LLM write reviewable source, then refine the result on your own machine.</p></div>
          <div class="hero-start"><Install /></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-terminal"><HeroJourney /></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <ProviderStrip />

    <Examples />

    <section class="manifesto section" data-reveal aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
      <div class="manifesto-main"><span class="eyebrow">02 / LESS GUESSWORK, MORE ENGINE</span><h2 id="manifesto-title">Describe the architecture.<br /><em>Let the engine place it.</em></h2><p>Give the model the architecture rather than a canvas full of coordinates. The engine handles placement and connector routes, producing a repeatable first render that you can adjust.</p><a class="button light" href={url('docs/routing/')}>Inside the engine <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
      <div class="manifesto-notes"><article><span class="mono">01 / DETERMINISTIC ROUTING</span><h3>The same source renders the same result.</h3><p>The document, catalog, and saved view determine the output. Routes are scored for obstacles, crossings, bends, and distance, with A* search when simple paths are blocked. Dense cases raise warnings so you know where the result needs review.</p></article><article><span class="mono">02 / YOUR FINAL SAY</span><h3>You can still adjust the result.</h3><p>Move a service, choose a port, or draw a manual route in Studio. Your positions and visual overrides stay in the <code>.dgm</code> file, so the next render preserves your decisions.</p></article><article><span class="mono">03 / LLM-FIRST, TOKEN-CONSCIOUS</span><h3>Use tokens for the architecture.</h3><p>Ask an agent to write compact source instead of issuing repeated shape-by-shape MCP tool calls. Letting the engine handle layout can reduce token use and make lighter models practical. Savings depend on the model, context, and workflow.</p></article></div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq section" aria-labelledby="faq-title"><div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">03 / PRACTICAL QUESTIONS</span><h2 id="faq-title">What to expect.</h2><p>A few things to know before you start.</p><div class="faq-brand" aria-hidden="true"><Image src={brandLogo} width={440} widths={[440, 880, 1320]} sizes="(max-width: 800px) 324px, 440px" quality={95} format="webp" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div></div><div class="faq-list">
      <details><summary>What does LLM-first diagrams as code mean?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Your architecture lives in a readable <code>.dgm</code> file, not a collection of hand-placed shapes. A large language model (LLM) can draft or update that source through a coding agent. The deterministic engine handles layout and routing. Review the diff in Git and refine the result in Studio. No built-in model or AI subscription is required. <a href={url('docs/agent-workflow/')}>Explore the LLM-first workflow.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need a coding agent?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>No, but one is useful for a first draft. Run <code>dumbdiagrams init</code> from the directory where you want the new project folder, then give the agent <code>AGENTS.md</code> and the shared <code>.dumbdiagrams/</code> instructions. Codex, Claude Code, Cursor, Gemini CLI, or another coding agent can write a solid starting point. You can also write <code>.dgm</code> files yourself or edit them in Studio.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Where do my diagrams live?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>On your machine, as portable <code>.dgm</code> files wherever you choose. Studio runs locally at <code>127.0.0.1</code> and saves the selected file directly; it is not tied to the repository where you started it. If you use the standalone browser editor instead, it keeps work in browser storage until you download a copy. <a href={url('docs/studio/')}>More about the two modes.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use it for clouds other than AWS?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. AWS was the starting point, and the catalog now includes Azure, Google Cloud, and cloud-agnostic assets too. Support for those may be less polished than the AWS workflow. If something you need for your provider is missing, contributions are welcome. See <a href={url('docs/contributing/')}>how to get involved</a>.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is this different from a whiteboard?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>You describe the services, boundaries, and connections, and Dumb Diagrams lays them out for you. You can then refine the result. The engine keeps placement, icon alignment, and arrow routing consistent, so an agent’s draft won’t leave you with drifting icons or arrows taking odd paths. An agent may give you a different draft each time, but the same <code>.dgm</code> source always renders the same way. The layout rules mostly follow AWS architecture guidance, with a few design choices of our own. It’s built for architecture diagrams, so it won’t cover general illustration, track your live infrastructure, or give you a hosted workspace for editing together.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is it free and open source?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Yes. Dumb Diagrams is released under AGPL-3.0-only. The provider icons come with their own terms. You’ll find the details in the <a href={url('LICENSE.txt')}>project license</a> and <a href={url('ICON-NOTICES.txt')}>icon notices</a>.</p></details>
    </div></section>

    <section class="closing" data-reveal><div><span class="eyebrow">START WITH ONE DIAGRAM</span><h2>Put the architecture<br /><em>on the page.</em></h2></div><a href={url('docs/quick-start/')} class="closing-cta trace-border" data-trace>Create your first diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
  </main>
</Base>
